              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_caption",
              "index": 5,
              "bbox": [
                0.46,
                0.322,
                0.539,
                0.34
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 3.12"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.365,
            0.809,
            0.388
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3.6 Real Numbers: Decimals and Cyclic Patterns"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.398,
            0.886,
            0.457
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "When we unite the dense web of Rational Numbers with the unfillable gaps of Irrational Numbers, we create the unbroken, continuous line of the Real Numbers (R)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.457,
            0.885,
            0.497
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The easiest way to distinguish a rational number from an irrational number is to look at its decimal expansion."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.52,
            0.772,
            0.542
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3.6.1 Rational Decimals: Terminating and Repeating"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.549,
            0.884,
            0.59
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "If you divide the numerator of a rational number by its denominator, exactly one of two things will happen:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.592,
            0.883,
            0.631
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "It terminates:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " The division eventually leaves a remainder of 0. The decimal stops."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.167,
            0.637,
            0.884,
            0.685
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Example "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "2:\\frac{3}{8}=0.375"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . (Can you tell for which rational numbers the decimal will be terminating?)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.686,
            0.883,
            0.727
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "It repeats:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " The division never reaches a remainder of 0, but the sequence of digits begins to loop infinitely."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.168,
            0.728,
            0.537,
            0.762
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Example "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "3:\\frac{5}{11}=0.454545\\cdots=0.\\overline{45}."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.78,
            0.399,
            0.803
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Think and Reflect"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.818,
            0.854,
            0.87
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Try to find the decimal expansions of "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac { 1 0 } { 3 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac { 1 1 } { 1 2 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " What do you observe about the repetition of the digits after the decimal point?"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
